Bug summary: PoissonDistributionImpl's constructor PoissonDistributionImpl(double p, NormalDistribution z) calls overridable setters (setNormal, setMean), which is unsafe because a subclass can override them. It also stores the caller-supplied NormalDistribution reference and later mutates it, so the caller's object is modified unexpectedly. The suggested fix is to make the class immutable and create the NormalDistribution internally.

Concrete examples:
[{'input': 'A subclass of PoissonDistributionImpl overrides setMean(double) or setNormal(NormalDistribution), then is constructed with new Sub(4.0, normal).', 'expected': "The constructor initializes state without invoking overridable methods, so the subclass override can't run on a partly constructed object.", 'actual': 'The constructor calls the overridable setNormal(z) and setMean(p). The subclass override runs before the subclass is initialized and can cause incorrect state or errors.'}, {'input': 'NormalDistribution z = new NormalDistributionImpl(0, 1); PoissonDistributionImpl d = new PoissonDistributionImpl(100.0, z); then use d, for example d.cumulativeProbability(...).', 'expected': "The caller's z is not modified, because the Poisson distribution uses its own internal NormalDistribution.", 'actual': "d keeps the reference to z and changes its mean and standard deviation when the mean is set. The caller's z is silently changed, and later changes to z outside the class also affect d."}]

